    "narrative": "LawDog enters today\u2019s slate with a high-ceiling core anchored by **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, who commands an elite 78.31 score. His matchup against Singer is boosted by a stellar 37-PA BvP history and a favorable #2 order slot, making him our primary offensive engine. **Lawrence Butler** (73.47) and **Taylor Ward** (70.65) also project as strong starts; **Lawrence Butler** benefits immensely from the platoon advantage against Houser and favorable park factors, while **Taylor Ward** leverages his lead-off spot and a strong platoon edge against Lovelady to maximize his potential for runs scored. **Geraldo Perdomo** (66.75) rounds out our top-tier plays, as his placement in the #3 spot combined with a park upgrade makes him a highly efficient asset for today\u2019s 5x5 needs.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Bryson Stott**, who hovered near the 45.52 mark but was ultimately squeezed out. Despite a decent platoon look against a right-hander, the optimization engine heavily penalized the matchup against Skenes\u2014whose sub-3.00 xERA acts as a significant drag on projected efficiency. We opted to prioritize the high-variance, park-advantaged profiles of **Drew Gilbert** and **Jeremiah Jackson** to preserve our plate appearance caps. While **Drew Gilbert** and **Jeremiah Jackson** face tougher order positioning, their path to scoring is less obstructed by elite-tier pitching than what **Bryson Stott** would have faced today.\n\nManaging risk remains critical, particularly with **Daulton Varsho** (63.22), whose score is suppressed by a 5-point wind-in penalty at the park, despite his otherwise strong platoon setup against Flaherty. **Masyn Winn** remains a marginal start (53.04) and is being monitored closely; while he provides stability, his lack of high-end power upside is somewhat mitigated by the favorable wind direction. We are comfortable with **Jonathan Aranda** (56.16) and **Luis Arraez** (60.42) as reliable producers, 